---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  DungeonIntro,
  Crossroads,
  DFSLocal,
  DFSGlobal,
  BFSLocal,
  BFSGlobal,
  Comparison,
  MapEditor,
} from "../../components/cave/dungeon-escape";
import { LogoAnimation } from "../../components/cave/intro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Escaping a Dungeon"
  description="Why breadth-first search (BFS) finds the shortest path and depth-first search (DFS) does not"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Imagine you are trapped in a dungeon. There is an exit somewhere in this
        maze. Finding any way out would already help. But if every step matters,
        then not all paths are equally good.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the real question is not just how to escape, but how to find the
        shortest way out.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DungeonIntro client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <LogoAnimation
      client:only="react"
      width={2080}
      height={600}
      pixelScale={8}
      duration={2700}
      loop={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let us zoom into a single crossroads. From here, we can move in several
        directions. So how should we explore? One possibility is: pick one
        direction and keep going for as long as possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose we always try going up first. There may be other options nearby,
        but for now we ignore them. We keep moving forward as long as the path
        is still open.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Crossroads client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section id="wip">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p></p>
      <p>
        Only when we get stuck do we go back to the last place where another
        path was still unexplored and we follow the next direction in our
        priority list. This strategy is called depth-first search.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DFSLocal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 4: Visited nodes -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To make this work, we also need to remember which positions we have
        already visited. Otherwise, in a maze with loops, we could wander in
        circles forever.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, in this position we have already visited the top cell, so
        we move right instead, that is our priority number 2 and it's still
        available.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem is that depth-first search can commit very early to a bad
        path. It might eventually find an exit, but through a long detour.
      </p>
      <p>
        And yes, if we had chosen a different movement priority, for example,
        taking always the down direction if available, maybe it would have
        gotten lucky and reached the optimal solution. But that is exactly the
        point: depth-first search has no guarantee.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DFSGlobal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 6: BFS local behavior -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>There is another classic strategy.</p>
      <p>
        Instead of committing to one path, we explore every position that is one
        step away from the start.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then every position that is two steps away. Then three. Then four. This
        is breadth-first search.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <BFSLocal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Breadth-first search expands layer by layer, so it reaches the nearest
        exit first.
      </p>
      <p>
        By the time breadth-first search reaches a position, it has already
        explored every shorter path to it. That is why the first exit it finds
        must be part of a shortest path.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <BFSGlobal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 8: Side-by-side comparison -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>Both algorithms need to remember visited positions.</p>
      <p>But they store very different things while searching.</p>
      <p>
        Depth-first search mostly keeps the current path in memory, plus the set
        of visited nodes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Breadth-first search must keep the whole frontier in memory, plus the
        visited nodes, and enough information to reconstruct the final path.
      </p>
      <p>
        So breadth-first search usually spends more memory in exchange for an
        important guarantee.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the trade-off is simple. Depth-first search uses less memory, but it
        may return a long path. Breadth-first search uses more memory, but it
        guarantees the shortest path. Escaping is easy. Escaping efficiently is
        the real puzzle.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Comparison client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Summary -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>Of course, these are not the only ways to search.</p>
      <p>
        But breadth-first search and depth-first search are two of the most
        classic starting points, and they already reveal a very important trade-
        off between memory and optimality.
      </p>
      <p>We will look at other search strategies in future videos.</p>
      <p>
        This was today's Cheese Byte! Remember, the code matters, but the idea
        comes first.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <h2 style="margin-bottom: 1em;">DFS vs BFS</h2>
    <div
      style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2em; text-align: left; font-size: 0.85em;"
    >
      <div>
        <h3>Depth-First Search</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Explores one path as deep as possible</li>
          <li>Uses less memory (only the current path)</li>
          <li>No guarantee of shortest path</li>
          <li>Result depends on direction priority</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Breadth-First Search</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Explores layer by layer</li>
          <li>Uses more memory (entire frontier)</li>
          <li>Guarantees the shortest path</li>
          <li>Independent of direction priority</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5em; font-style: italic; opacity: 0.7;">
      Escaping is easy. Escaping efficiently is the real puzzle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Map Editor (helper slide for designing maps) -->
  <section data-visibility="hidden">
    <MapEditor client:only="react" />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
